WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.610
What does our linear model
receive when it predicts an hour?

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:08.290
Take Monday, the third of January,
twenty eleven, at eight in the morning.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.410
This is training record fifty four.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:14.670
Under the retained convention,
the request is issued at seven.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.950
It predicts observed rentals
during eight to nine.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.510
The recorded outcome is one
hundred and fifty four rentals,

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.330
available after that interval.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.650
Calendar information is
available in advance.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:31.260
The target and observed target hour
weather are excluded from prediction

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:32.110
inputs.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.580
We are following a real
training observation,

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:38.550
with the same information boundary
used by the earlier baselines.

00:00:40.957 --> 00:00:44.767
The raw vector has forty five
columns in a fixed order.

00:00:45.277 --> 00:00:46.867
Hour eight, Monday,

00:00:47.917 --> 00:00:48.217
January

00:00:48.617 --> 00:00:50.507
and the working day flag are active.

00:00:51.017 --> 00:00:54.307
Every other entry is zero,
including the holiday flag.

00:00:55.297 --> 00:00:57.607
Record identity is stored separately.

00:00:58.677 --> 00:01:03.457
A raw coordinate linear forecast adds an
intercept and the coefficients for these

00:01:03.457 --> 00:01:04.507
active inputs.

00:01:04.997 --> 00:01:07.607
Each adjustment is shared
by many observations.

00:01:08.697 --> 00:01:13.577
Expanding the hour into separate
indicators lets eight and two receive

00:01:13.577 --> 00:01:18.467
different effects, without assuming a
constant increase for every hour number.

00:01:19.057 --> 00:01:24.517
Multiplying columns in the wrong order can
produce a number with valid dimensions and

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:25.527
the wrong meaning.

00:01:26.237 --> 00:01:28.727
The saved feature order
is part of the predictor.

00:01:30.983 --> 00:01:33.953
The training loop uses
standardized coordinates.

00:01:34.823 --> 00:01:35.653
For each column,

00:01:35.743 --> 00:01:40.713
subtract its training mean and divide
by its population standard deviation.

00:01:41.563 --> 00:01:46.563
The hour eight indicator has a training
mean near zero point zero four two,

00:01:46.563 --> 00:01:50.153
and a scale near zero point two zero one.

00:01:50.663 --> 00:01:52.413
For our active indicator,

00:01:52.803 --> 00:01:55.913
one minus that mean, divided by the scale,

00:01:56.103 --> 00:01:59.093
is about four point seven seven six six.

00:01:59.783 --> 00:02:02.673
Inactive columns become
negative after centering.

00:02:03.243 --> 00:02:05.993
We must use the full standardized vector,

00:02:06.123 --> 00:02:09.413
rather than only its four
originally active entries.

00:02:10.123 --> 00:02:13.553
The transformation changes numerical
coordinates for optimization.

00:02:14.263 --> 00:02:18.373
It adds no information and leaves
the target in rentals per hour.

00:02:20.442 --> 00:02:26.192
The source computes one mean and one
population deviation per feature column.

00:02:26.682 --> 00:02:29.252
Axis zero reduces over training rows.

00:02:29.802 --> 00:02:32.232
The denominator is the
full training count.

00:02:32.742 --> 00:02:37.632
A constant column receives scale one,
protecting against division by zero.

00:02:38.202 --> 00:02:40.492
It then maps to zero on training.

00:02:41.002 --> 00:02:43.792
The model saves these
values beside its weights.

00:02:44.282 --> 00:02:48.452
Prediction transforms new raw
inputs with the saved statistics,

00:02:48.942 --> 00:02:51.572
then applies the dot
product and intercept.

00:02:52.122 --> 00:02:54.732
The real training
columns are not constant,

00:02:55.222 --> 00:02:57.352
but a small fixture checks that policy.

00:02:57.982 --> 00:03:01.632
Standardization does not remove
dependent indicator columns.

00:03:02.042 --> 00:03:05.802
It makes the gradient coordinates
more comparable while preserving the

00:03:05.802 --> 00:03:07.452
representable linear function.

00:03:09.567 --> 00:03:13.977
Suppose a later request batch
contains mostly eight oclock hours.

00:03:14.367 --> 00:03:17.277
Should we recompute the
feature mean from that batch?

00:03:17.787 --> 00:03:19.897
We keep the saved training mean.

00:03:20.107 --> 00:03:22.257
The weights were fitted
in those coordinates.

00:03:22.807 --> 00:03:27.567
A new batch mean would change the meaning
of the same coefficient and could make one

00:03:27.567 --> 00:03:31.157
forecast depend on the other
requests submitted beside it.

00:03:31.587 --> 00:03:32.297
Likewise,

00:03:32.467 --> 00:03:37.527
assigning scale one to a constant training
feature does not teach its effect for a

00:03:37.527 --> 00:03:38.797
value never observed.

00:03:39.307 --> 00:03:40.717
That remains limited evidence.

00:03:41.467 --> 00:03:43.357
The raw exports stay unchanged.

00:03:43.967 --> 00:03:45.717
The scaler is learned state,

00:03:45.967 --> 00:03:50.317
fitted only on training and reused
consistently for every later prediction.

